<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Babylonia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Babylonia was the region of southern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> centred on the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. Its fertile irrigated land made it one of the wealthiest regions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a>. It was ruled successively by the Babylonian kings, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldeans</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babylonia.jpg" alt="Babylonia" width="320" height="278" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hammurabi%27s_Babylonia_1.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>This tolerance for local institutions was a deliberate strategy: Cyrus framed the conquest of Babylon as the restoration of Marduk's favour, and the Achaemenid kings consistently sought legitimacy within the religious frameworks of their subjects, ruling as pharaoh in Egypt and as restorer of religious order in <b>Babylonia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Kanesh document Assyrian merchants bringing tin from the east, probably from Iran and Central Asia, and textiles from <b>Babylonia</b> and Assur itself, exchanging them for Anatolian silver and gold; modern estimates suggest that around 100 tons of tin and some 100,000 textiles passed through the network over the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…craftsmen and hire rates for oxen, boats and carts, regulate interest on loans of grain and silver, define contracts for partnerships and deposits, and set out the obligations of merchants, tavern keepers and boatmen — offering a remarkably detailed window into the commercial life of early second-millennium <b>Babylonia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The deportation policy, most famously applied to Judah in 597 and 587/586 BCE, served an economic as well as a political purpose: craftsmen, administrators and labourers were resettled in <b>Babylonia</b> to supply the enormous building programmes under way there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1450 BCE Kassite <b>Babylonia</b> brought Dilmun under direct rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The archives in Akkadian and other languages are equally valuable for Late Bronze Age diplomacy, law and trade, documenting how a small kingdom worked within the international system of Egypt, Hatti and <b>Babylonia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ugarit">Kingdom of Ugarit · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
